Distributed Job Scheduler is one of the most common interview questions, and during my tenure at Walmart, I designed and developed it in production.

What most people don't get to see is what it looks like once it's actually running in production, handling retries, crashes, and cancellations at scale.

Here's how the pieces fit together:

𝐆𝐞𝐭𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚 𝐣𝐨𝐛 𝐢𝐧𝐭𝐨 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐬𝐲𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐦:
𝑨𝑷𝑰 𝑮𝒂𝒕𝒆𝒘𝒂𝒚 - Auth, rate limiting, and routing sit here before a request ever touches internal services.
𝑱𝒐𝒃 𝑺𝒆𝒓𝒗𝒊𝒄𝒆 - owns create/edit/schedule/cancel and publishes every job execution event to Kafka. Nothing executes without going through this path first.
𝑱𝒐𝒃 𝑺𝒆𝒂𝒓𝒄𝒉 𝑺𝒆𝒓𝒗𝒊𝒄𝒆 - a separate read path so users can look up jobs by jobId or status without hitting the write-heavy Job Service.

𝐆𝐞𝐭𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚 𝐣𝐨𝐛 𝐭𝐨 𝐚𝐜𝐭𝐮𝐚𝐥𝐥𝐲 𝐫𝐮𝐧:
𝑾𝒂𝒕𝒄𝒉𝒆𝒓 𝑺𝒆𝒓𝒗𝒊𝒄𝒆 - polls every 10–20 sec for jobs scheduled in [now, now+5 min] and flips them to Queued. It's also the first line of failure detection: any "running" job whose modified_time is stale by 10–15 sec is assumed dead.
𝑬𝒙𝒆𝒄𝒖𝒕𝒐𝒓 𝑺𝒆𝒓𝒗𝒊𝒄𝒆 - runs the job (Python scripts, inside Docker) and pushes failures straight to Kafka for retry.
𝑫𝒐𝒄𝒌𝒆𝒓 𝑬𝒙𝒆𝒄𝒖𝒕𝒊𝒐𝒏 𝑳𝒂𝒚𝒆𝒓 - every job gets its own container. Fault isolation and a consistent runtime, without one bad script taking down a shared process.
𝒁𝒐𝒐𝒌𝒆𝒆𝒑𝒆𝒓 - heartbeats from executors, used for health checks and cluster coordination.

𝐖𝐡𝐞𝐧 𝐭𝐡𝐢𝐧𝐠𝐬 𝐟𝐚𝐢𝐥:
𝑹𝒆𝒕𝒓𝒚 𝑴𝒆𝒄𝒉𝒂𝒏𝒊𝒔𝒎 - failed executions land on a Kafka retry topic, retry count gets bumped in the DB.
𝑭𝒂𝒊𝒍𝒖𝒓𝒆 𝑫𝒆𝒕𝒆𝒄𝒕𝒊𝒐𝒏 — the hard case isn't the job that fails cleanly, it's the executor that crashes mid-job and never gets to report anything. Stale modified_time on a "running" job is the only signal you get — so the Watcher treats it as a failure and moves on.

𝐊𝐢𝐥𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚 𝐣𝐨𝐛 𝐨𝐧 𝐩𝐮𝐫𝐩𝐨𝐬𝐞:
𝑪𝒂𝒏𝒄𝒆𝒍 𝑱𝒐𝒃 𝑭𝒍𝒐𝒘 - future jobs are canceled via a Kafka event before they're ever queued. Running jobs are harder to manage: you can't unpublish a job that's already executing.
𝑹𝒆𝒅𝒊𝒔 - executors poll Redis for a cancel signal mid-run. TTL on the key means a stale cancel request expires instead of haunting a job that already finished.

𝐌𝐚𝐤𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐢𝐭 𝐪𝐮𝐞𝐫𝐲𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞:
𝑱𝒐𝒃 𝑪𝒐𝒏𝒔𝒖𝒎𝒆𝒓 𝑺𝒆𝒓𝒗𝒊𝒄𝒆 - consumes the Kafka events and is the only writer of status, retry count, and execution metadata into the database. One writer, one source of truth.
𝑪𝒂𝒔𝒔𝒂𝒏𝒅𝒓𝒂 - user_id as partition key, job_id as clustering key. Job lookups per user stay fast no matter how wide the table gets.
𝑺3 - scripts, files, and artifacts a job needs at execution time live here, not in the DB.

It's a small interview topic that hides a genuinely hard production problem: detecting failures, retrying safely, and cancelling something that's already running.




